  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to optimize the step function using a multi-scale hierarchical approach combined with advanced optimization techniques. Unlike the previous algorithm, we move away from purely binary representations and incorporate continuous-relaxation phases within the search process to escape local optima. This approach aims to:\n   - Maintain population diversity with adaptive perturbations\n   - Balance global exploration (Atkin exploration) and local exploitation (Penrose-inspired local refinement)\n   - Use both discrete and continuous representations\n   - Maintain single-array state throughout transformations\n   \n   The algorithm's effectiveness comes from:\n   - Introducing multi-step repair mechanisms\n   - Intrinsic inversions of the search process at multiple levels\n   - Information-theoretic phrasing of the optimization problem\n   - Incorporation of derivatives for directed local refinement\n   \n   Key limitations include the high-dimensional Fourier domain, but we mitigate this through hierarchical projections. We distinguish this approach through its use of continuous relaxation, dimensionally-aware moves, and refreshment mechanisms.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nFor representation: \n   - 600-point grid dividing [0,2]\n   - Binary/continuous convex hull representation\n   - Fourier coefficients with the constraint |h[i]|=1\n   - Autocorrelation computed via Fourier transform\n   - Smoothed optimization objective with logarithmic barrier\n   - Derivatives incorporated for gradient descent\n\nConstrained optimization (using MOSEK):\n   - Variance minimization with logarithmic penalty\n   - Projection back to [0,1] using entropy minimization\n   - Fourier-domain moves with frequency localization\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - Initialize with population of 20-30 individuals\n   - Phase 1: Coarse resolution search\n   - Phase 2: Penrose duality and Fourier transform pruning\n   - Phase 3: Local gradient refinement with Lipschitz constraints\n   - Population updates with Atkin-inspired magnification\n   - Manual repair and projection\n   - Strict dimension-maintaining operations\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Atkin parameters: dynamically adjusted based on neighborhood radius\n   - Penrose coefficient: 0.3-0.8 for local magnitude\n   - Fourier dimension: 60-200\n   - Relief schedule: distance-dependent inversions\n   - MOSEKsolver configured for log-dual barrier\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   - Detailed time estimation per phase\n   - Internal budget checks in each reduction segment\n   - Three-tier fallback system on deterioration\n   - Specialized branch-handling for Fourier counterpart\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Error-checking projection\n   - Final objective recomputation\n   - Four-constraint satisfaction check\n   - Agnostic to parent grid size",
